title: Plan Your Stay: One Week in Heidelberg
---
author: ORGANIZERS
---
pub_date: 2026-11-23
---
body:

PyCon DE 2027 is more than three conference days. It's a whole week in Heidelberg, and the best way to experience it is to arrive early and stay late. Here's what the week looks like, so you can book your travel once.

## The week at a glance

- **Monday, 19 April:** [Masterclasses](/masterclasses/) and [Django Girls](/satellite-events/), early badge pickup in the afternoon
- **Tuesday–Thursday, 20–22 April:** Conference at the Heidelberg Congress Center
- **Friday–Saturday, 23–24 April:** [Sprints](/sprints/), [hackathons](/satellite-events/) and Beginners' Day
- **Sunday, 25 April:** Heidelberg is yours

## Arrive on Sunday: Masterclasses on Monday

Monday is a full day of hands-on [Masterclasses](/masterclasses/) in small groups, the perfect warm-up before the talks begin. Full-day classes run 9:00–16:00, half-day classes run in the morning or afternoon. Topics range from [pytest](/masterclasses/pytest-tips-and-tricks-freya-bruhin/), [uv](/masterclasses/fast-python-development-with-uv-mike-mueller/) and [Rust for Python developers](/masterclasses/rust-for-python-developers-rodrigo-girao-serrao/) to [vector search](/masterclasses/architecting-vector-search-clelia-astra-bertelli-evgeniya-sukhodolskaya/), [databases](/masterclasses/connecting-your-backend-to-a-database-kristian-rother/) and [leading under pressure](/masterclasses/leading-under-pressure-tereza-iofciu/), with more to come.

Masterclasses are booked separately from your conference pass, so plan to be in Heidelberg on Sunday evening.

## Three conference days

Tuesday to Thursday belong to the talks, tutorials, the hallway track and the social event on Wednesday evening. This is the heart of the week. The social event is a ticket add-on with limited space, [see the FAQ](/faqs/#social-event-tickets).

Already in town on Monday? Pick up your badge early in the afternoon and skip the queue on Tuesday morning. Times to be confirmed.

## Stay for the sprints

Don't jump on the train on Thursday evening. On Friday and Saturday, the [sprints](/sprints/) turn three days of listening into two days of building. Sit next to maintainers of scikit-learn, spaCy, Project Jupyter and many more, and make a contribution that ships. First-timers are more than welcome: project leads bring beginner-friendly issues and help you get started.

[Hackathons](/satellite-events/) run on the same days. The sprints need a separate registration, which we'll announce soon.

## Stay the weekend

You've come all the way to one of Germany's most beautiful cities: give it a day. Walk up to the castle, cross the Old Bridge over the Neckar, take the Philosophers' Walk for the view across the old town, or ride the funicular up to the Königstuhl. Saturday evening and Sunday are perfect for exploring Heidelberg with the friends you made during the week.

## Book early

Heidelberg is popular in spring, and a week-long stay is easier to book early. Secure your [conference pass](/buy-ticket/) and [Masterclass](/masterclasses/), then book your accommodation for the whole week. Need support with travel and accommodation? Apply to our [Financial Aid Programme](/financial-aid/) by 27 December.

[Get your ticket](/buy-ticket/)

See you in Heidelberg.

— The PyCon DE Team
